#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 720.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125311} :activity/id #uuid "2bfe5aae-e1d5-50d4-96df-8e775e21203d" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048877 :topic/id #uuid "c8e11bf4-259c-5464-94a3-b5c5d6409c00" :topic/math-academy-id 1932} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125311 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125741 :tutorial/content "The partial derivative of a function exists where the original function is defined *and* the partial derivative is defined.\n\nFor example, let's consider the function $f(x,y) = \\ln(xy).$ We wish to determine the set of values $(x,y)$ on which the partial derivative $\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}$ exists.\n\nFirst, we need to find the domain of $f$ since we cannot differentiate $f$ at any point that lies outside of its domain. The natural logarithm is defined only for positive inputs, so the domain of $f$ is \n\n$$\nD_f = \\{ (x,y)\\, : \\, xy > 0 \\}.\n$$\n\nNext, we compute the partial derivative:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial x} = \\dfrac{1}{x}\n$$\n\nThe partial derivative $\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}$ (also denoted $f_x$) is not defined at $x=0.$ So, the domain of $f_x$ is\n\n$$\nD_{f_x} = \\{ (x,y)\\, : \\, x \\ne 0 \\}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the set where  $\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}$ exists, we take the intersection of the domain of the original function $(D_f)$ with the domain of the partial derivative $(D_{f_x})$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{f}\\cap {D}_{{f}_{x}} & =\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,xy>0\\}\\cap \\{(x,y)\\,:\\,x\\ne 0\\} \\\\  & =\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,xy>0\\}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}$ exists on the set $\\{ (x,y)\\, : \\, xy \\gt 0 \\}.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "5862db65-6f2d-5256-968d-cb971dd6b2f1" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2532 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "e4bac5fb-b77d-59b1-8380-2ff0cf754d5c" :step/math-academy-id 11425 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125312}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125312 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124465 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124269 :question/id #uuid "a663f68e-1fb9-5653-a128-1b55c2a58769" :question/math-academy-id "e-3449"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8702032e-0795-5b51-9cc9-f89b35b64fa0" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048707} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049984} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050367} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050850} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186051033}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124989} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125005} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268899} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268901} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323840} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339041} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339042} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186381041} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382186} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382200} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426721} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436747} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436748} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436750}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Domain Where a Partial Derivative Exists"} :step/id #uuid "d81b9ea9-a347-5ebb-91d1-4bc5c4b566e0" :step/math-academy-id 11426 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125463}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125313 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124466 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124270 :question/id #uuid "7297f213-2db2-5da2-9bba-cac7e31f6b64" :question/math-academy-id "e-3450"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9d090462-a031-5f96-ac13-90dac882edab" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124995} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125000} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268906} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268907} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268914} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335822} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339045} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186381055} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382214} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382221} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409557} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409558} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436752}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Points Where Partial Derivatives Exist"} :step/id #uuid "f4cc9d43-0b3e-5cd2-8e04-c1f0f43e9e2a" :step/math-academy-id 11429 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125314}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125314 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125742 :tutorial/content "From single-variable calculus, if a function $y=f(x)$ is differentiable at a point, then it is continuous at that point.\n\nBut for a multivariable function, even if all partial derivatives are defined at a certain point, it **does not mean** that the function is continuous at that point!\n\nThe reason why partial differentiability does not imply continuity in the case of a multivariable function is that\n\n- the existence of the partial derivatives $f_x$ and $f_y$ depend only on the behavior of $f$ parallel to the $x$- and $y$-axes, whereas\n\n- the continuity of $f$ depends on its behavior as we approach $(x_0,y_0)$ from any direction.\n\nFor example, let's consider the following multivariable function:\n\n![](images/cb/cb5aad599789d1e317ff3ddc07eaa418e80469307f67e98e2108660fc7d15701.svg)\n\nThe function $f$ is defined at the point $(0,0)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\nf(0,0)=0\n$$\n\nLikewise, the partial derivatives $f_x$ and $f_y$ are both defined at the point $(0,0).$ We can compute them using the limit definition of the partial derivative, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(0,0) & ={lim}_{h\\to 0}\\frac{1}{h}[f(0+h,0)-f(0,0)] \\\\  & ={lim}_{h\\to 0}\\frac{1}{h}[\\frac{(h)(0)}{{h}^{2}+{0}^{2}}-0] \\\\  & ={lim}_{h\\to 0}\\frac{1}{h}[0-0] \\\\  & =0 \\\\ {f}_{y}(0,0) & ={lim}_{h\\to 0}\\frac{1}{h}[f(0,0+h)-f(0,0)] \\\\  & ={lim}_{h\\to 0}\\frac{1}{h}[\\frac{(0)(h)}{{0}^{2}+{h}^{2}}-0] \\\\  & ={lim}_{h\\to 0}\\frac{1}{h}[0-0] \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the partial derivatives both exist at $(0,0).$ We will now show that the function is not continuous at $(0,0).$\n\nFor the function $f$ to be continuous at $(0,0),$ the limit of the function must be equal to the function value, regardless of the path taken. So, we must have\n\n$$\n\\lim\\limits_{(x,y) \\to (0,0)}  f(x,y) = f(0,0) =0.\n$$\n\nHowever, this is not the case here. When we evaluate the limit along the path $y=x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{(x,y)\\to (0,0)\\,\\,y=x}f(x,y) & ={lim}_{y\\to 0}\\frac{x\\cdot x}{{x}^{2}+{x}^{2}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{y\\to 0}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2{x}^{2}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{y\\to 0}\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & \\ne 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our function $f(x,y)$ is *not* continuous at the point $(0,0),$ even though both of its partial derivatives exist there." :tutorial/id #uuid "ca47070b-a1d3-53ad-8f57-2dbd4e770dce" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2533 :tutorial/title "Partial Differentiability Does Not Imply Continuity"} :step/id #uuid "f23e88d8-c204-5795-b172-b05000a202c3" :step/math-academy-id 11432} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125463 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124557 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124366 :question/id #uuid "97192aac-6c1a-5ead-962b-0bf7ab573661" :question/math-academy-id "e-6109"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e2d7d4db-e9e1-54a0-aadb-42447771855d" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125001} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125193} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268902} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268903} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268904} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339043} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339044} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339046} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339047} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186381048} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382207} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409559} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426720} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436749} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436751}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Domain Where All Partial Derivatives Exist"} :step/id #uuid "c86dfe84-cedf-55cf-bf44-efb12a49ac66" :step/math-academy-id 22675 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125313}}] :activity/title "Partial Differentiability of Multivariable Functions" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186124725}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046659} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409562} :activity/id #uuid "59747c22-b269-5844-bdda-f1750192841c" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048877 :topic/id #uuid "c8e11bf4-259c-5464-94a3-b5c5d6409c00" :topic/math-academy-id 1932} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409560 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409558 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "899f43e4-fc9a-5506-b84d-a25c2d9eb6f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453928} {:answer/id #uuid "ec4690b4-12f7-5ffd-a0f0-d137fa95703c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453929}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186453928} :answer-field/id #uuid "2ccf0ae7-dc64-51f0-892e-8d88c6910e82" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454714} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "376cf739-f8d8-5cb0-91a0-52b41ea250c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453930} {:answer/id #uuid "ffcc07c3-7704-591c-aebf-ff0249e9dd10" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453931}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186453931} :answer-field/id #uuid "72f29120-e8c2-52fc-abd7-8386d5f878c4" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454715} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fc77c615-3280-58d4-a19d-18282a3f7486" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453932} {:answer/id #uuid "8cfc9ce4-4706-5c6b-8f71-059b43d404d7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453933}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186453932} :answer-field/id #uuid "a7c89136-ff34-5898-9f58-5a98f17bcfb1" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454716}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "538fdcf0-8dcd-525c-becd-0317742dbf1a" :question/math-academy-id "q-72010" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true about the function $f(x,y)={e}^{\\sqrt{xy}}?$\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** Both partial derivatives exist at the point $(1,-1)$\n\n- **II.** ${f}_{y}(-1,-1)$ is defined\n\n- **III.** ${f}_{x}(0,1)$ is defined" :question/worked-solution "First, we compute the partial derivatives of $f(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & ={e}^{\\sqrt{xy}}\\cdot \\frac{y}{2\\sqrt{xy}} \\\\  & =\\frac{y{e}^{\\sqrt{xy}}}{2\\sqrt{xy}} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & ={e}^{\\sqrt{xy}}\\cdot \\frac{x}{2\\sqrt{xy}} \\\\  & =\\frac{x{e}^{\\sqrt{xy}}}{2\\sqrt{xy}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's examine the statements one-by-one. \n\n- Statement I is false. If we substitute $(1,-1)$ into $f$, we get\n\n$$\nf(1,-1)={e}^{\\sqrt{(1)(-1)}}=-{e}^{\\sqrt{-1}}\n$$\n\nwhich is undefined since we have a negative number under the radical.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, the function $f$ is defined at $(-1,-1)$, where we have \n\n$$\nf(-1,-1)={e}^{\\sqrt{(-1)(-1)}}={e}^{\\sqrt{1}}={e}^{1}=e,\n$$\n\nand ${f}_{y}$ is also defined at $(-1,-1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(-1,-1) & =\\frac{(-1){e}^{\\sqrt{(-1)(-1)}}}{2\\sqrt{(-1)(-1)}}=-\\frac{{e}^{\\sqrt{1}}}{2\\sqrt{1}}=-\\frac{e}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. If we substitute $(0,1)$ into ${f}_{x}$, we get\n\n$$\n{f}_{x}(0,1)=1\\cdot \\frac{{e}^{\\sqrt{0\\cdot 1}}}{2\\sqrt{0\\cdot 1}}=\\frac{{e}^{0}}{2\\sqrt{0}}=\\frac{1}{0},\n$$\n\nwhich is undefined since we have division by zero.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is 'II  only'."} :step/id #uuid "06a4b700-aa67-595f-ad77-c3a9c2d08fa3" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409563}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409561 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339043 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8b4579a6-884f-5620-9c48-03f859207fbd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}>0}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337292} {:answer/id #uuid "aa211335-2ec0-5533-aaf0-0747d0111e9a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\ge 0}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337566} {:answer/id #uuid "ab12ccaa-59a3-5391-8885-0e57cfa9220d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}<0}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337575} {:answer/id #uuid "d30b5af1-be96-52a2-983e-53d94d787930" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\ne 0}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337875} {:answer/id #uuid "d932ef34-432a-58eb-96dc-98456b7b3465" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=0}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337921}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186337292} :answer-field/id #uuid "167ce942-9903-5739-b46e-634adbd1db2c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338315}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "329fb25a-c39e-587a-8c51-720f867dc9e6" :question/math-academy-id "q-72005" :question/problem "Given that $g(x,y)=x\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})$ and\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂g}{∂x}=\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}),\\,\\frac{∂g}{∂y}=\\frac{2xy}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}},\n$$\n\nfind the set of values $(x,y)$ where all partial derivatives of $g$ exist." :question/worked-solution "First, notice that $g(x,y)=x\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})$ is defined only when ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}>0.$ So, the domain of $g$ is \n\n$$\n{D}_{g}={(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}>0}.\n$$\n\nAs for the partial derivatives, we have the following.\n\n- The function ${g}_{x}(x,y)=\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})$ is defined only when ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}>0.$ So, the domain of ${g}_{x}$ is \n\n$$\n{D}_{{g}_{x}}={(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}>0}.\n$$\n\n- The function ${g}_{y}(x,y)=\\frac{2xy}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}$ is defined only when ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\ne 0.$ So, the domain of ${g}_{y}$ is \n\n$$\n{D}_{{g}_{y}}={(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\ne 0}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that the partials of $g$ exist on the set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{g}\\cap {D}_{{g}_{x}}\\cap {D}_{{g}_{y}} & ={(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}>0}\\cap {(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}>0}\\cap {(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\ne 0} \\\\  & ={(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}>0}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "1786d23c-6b2b-5b25-8a34-a3df54af58bf" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409560}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409562 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335822 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "39c4f7c1-2cc1-57ed-a18b-bf91992e00d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451483} {:answer/id #uuid "f3e4c2de-8568-5ff2-baf8-40d15c25b5ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451484}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186451484} :answer-field/id #uuid "99c60c75-d597-5852-8b29-5420ba46e80b" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452589} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "96e8d67b-c006-521e-ad4e-1b3b5e5bbae0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451485} {:answer/id #uuid "5fcff0a4-c6d8-5ff7-9344-ecba3634e8b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451486}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186451485} :answer-field/id #uuid "bf8a887b-1bb0-5b36-af98-54f46b4ff833" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452590} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1bd09d87-8f09-5958-88e2-49043d0205b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451487} {:answer/id #uuid "72089ef2-d948-531c-b1f3-ac84e9dc1b0d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451488}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186451488} :answer-field/id #uuid "6d4fe7e1-7135-5cca-a3a0-20d39be1b879" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452591}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "b22bd39f-49d5-56c7-8797-705e83876e55" :question/math-academy-id "q-65255" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true about the function $f(x,y)=xy{e}^{x/y}?$\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** ${f}_{x}(0,1)$is defined\n\n- **II.** ${f}_{y}(1,0)$ is defined\n\n- **III.** Both partial derivatives exist at the point $(1,1)$" :question/worked-solution "First, we compute the partial derivatives of $f(x,y):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(xy{e}^{x/y}) \\\\  & =y{e}^{x/y}+xy{e}^{x/y}(\\frac{1}{y}) \\\\  & =y{e}^{x/y}+x{e}^{x/y} \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(xy{e}^{x/y}) \\\\  & =x{e}^{x/y}+xy{e}^{x/y}(-\\frac{x}{{y}^{2}}) \\\\  & =x{e}^{x/y}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{y}{e}^{x/y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's examine the statements one-by-one.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, the function $f$ itself is defined at $(0,1)$, where we have \n\n$$\nf(0,1)=0\\cdot 1\\cdot {e}^{0/1}=0,\n$$\n\nand ${f}_{x}$ is also defined at $(0,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(0,1)=1\\cdot {e}^{0/1}+0\\cdot {e}^{0/1}=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. If we substitute $(1,0)$ into ${f}_{y}$, we get\n\n$$\n{f}_{y}(1,0)=1\\cdot {e}^{1/0}-\\frac{{1}^{2}}{0}\\cdot {e}^{1/0},\n$$\n\nwhich is undefined since we have division by zero.\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, the function $f$ is defined at $(1,1)$, where we have \n\n$$\nf(1,1)=1\\cdot 1\\cdot {e}^{1/1}=e,\n$$\n\nand both partial derivatives are also defined at $(1,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(1,1) & =1\\cdot {e}^{1/1}+1\\cdot {e}^{1/1}=2e \\\\ {f}_{y}(1,1) & =1\\cdot {e}^{1/1}-\\frac{{1}^{2}}{1}\\cdot {e}^{1/1}=e-e=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only\"."} :step/id #uuid "488b6ab6-cae5-5979-9b52-c3723c3f093b" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409561}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409563 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409559 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1625c29f-0f6c-51a9-9abf-da854c690be8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,xyz>0,\\,x+y+z\\ne 0\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445247} {:answer/id #uuid "603bfd17-5e73-595d-9e4e-67eb66547b83" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,xyz>0\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445251} {:answer/id #uuid "6e5cf9f7-9ed5-55ea-b3dd-342a00251b3b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,x\\ne 0,\\,y\\ne 0,\\,z\\ne 0\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445253} {:answer/id #uuid "b33e9175-1fe3-525c-84d8-993f2fb37971" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,x+y+z>0\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445255} {:answer/id #uuid "d2f2980b-11d7-556d-9928-b285bb77ebda" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,xyz\\ge 0\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445256}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186445251} :answer-field/id #uuid "c3c16b06-37a6-5eac-b3ba-318617c8302e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409555}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "5744243e-c58b-5537-916f-bee0cb473253" :question/math-academy-id "q-132654" :question/problem "Given that $f(x,y,z)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xyz)$ and\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=\\frac{1}{x},\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=\\frac{1}{y},\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂z}=\\frac{1}{z},\n$$\n\nfind the set of values $(x,y,z)$ on which all partial derivatives of $f$ exist." :question/worked-solution "First, notice that $f(x,y,z)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xyz)$ is defined only when $xyz>0.$ So, the domain of $f$ is \n\n$$\n{D}_{f}=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,xyz>0\\}.\n$$\n\nAs for the partial derivatives, we have the following.\n\n- The function $\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=\\frac{1}{x}$ is defined only when $x\\ne 0.$ So, the domain of ${f}_{x}$ is \n\n$$\n{D}_{{f}_{x}}=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,x\\ne 0\\}.\n$$\n\n- The function $\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=\\frac{1}{y}$ is defined only when $y\\ne 0.$ So, the domain of ${f}_{y}$ is \n\n$$\n{D}_{{f}_{y}}=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,y\\ne 0\\}.\n$$\n\n- The function $\\frac{∂f}{∂z}=\\frac{1}{z}$ is defined only when $z\\ne 0.$ So, the domain of ${f}_{z}$ is \n\n$$\n{D}_{{f}_{z}}=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,z\\ne 0\\}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that the partial derivatives of $f$ exist on the set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{f}\\cap {D}_{{f}_{x}}\\cap {D}_{{f}_{y}}\\cap {D}_{{f}_{z}} & =\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,xyz>0\\}\\cap \\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,x\\ne 0\\}\\cap \\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,y\\ne 0\\}\\cap \\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,z\\ne 0\\} \\\\  & =\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,xyz>0\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "61817e36-5704-54ae-b054-a7bf550b56c7" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409564}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409564 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409557 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e148fac7-727b-55d9-ba67-0a9fc7a276e4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453368} {:answer/id #uuid "6c6ec723-5cd2-5173-b8f1-1778f583295d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453369}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186453369} :answer-field/id #uuid "4e8e1392-18d1-56b3-b0f5-ff3dd861687a" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454434} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1a536093-cb71-5d44-a52f-8e05b4b9f74a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453370} {:answer/id #uuid "cc2d5ab2-a228-5241-aef5-decbffea282c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453371}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186453371} :answer-field/id #uuid "deacc4af-75f1-5a94-a0d0-5d1ad4ac3ea8" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454435} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "80b4f556-0cc0-5fc7-9fac-56ff9260d6c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453372} {:answer/id #uuid "cbd91732-9818-5742-9c5d-ea668d6dbe0a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453373}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186453373} :answer-field/id #uuid "7f7af479-38a3-58de-bca6-c137a88e2790" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454436}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "0a3b6388-98bd-5083-8d1c-f60a05890b23" :question/math-academy-id "q-132726" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true about the function $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{y})?$\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** Both partial derivatives exist at the point $(\\sqrt{3},1)$\n- **II.** ${f}_{x}(1,\\sqrt{3})$ is defined\n- **III.** ${f}_{y}(0,1)$ is defined" :question/worked-solution "First, we compute the partial derivatives of $f(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{y}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{(\\frac{x}{y})}^{2}+1}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{x}{y}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{y}^{2}}+1}\\cdot \\frac{1}{y} \\\\  & =\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{1}{y} \\\\  & =\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{y}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{(\\frac{x}{y})}^{2}+1}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{x}{y}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{y}^{2}}+1}\\cdot (-\\frac{x}{{y}^{2}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\cdot (-\\frac{x}{{y}^{2}}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's examine the statements one-by-one.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, the function $f$ is defined at $(\\sqrt{3},1)$, where we have \n\n$$\nf(\\sqrt{3},1)=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{1})=\\frac{\\pi }{3},\n$$\n\nand both partial derivatives are also defined at $(\\sqrt{3},1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(\\sqrt{3},1) & =\\frac{1}{(\\sqrt{3})^{2}+{1}^{2}}=\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ {f}_{y}(\\sqrt{3},1) & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{(\\sqrt{3})^{2}+{1}^{2}}=-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, the function $f$ itself is defined at $(1,\\sqrt{3})$, where we have \n\n$$\nf(1,\\sqrt{3})=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}})=\\frac{\\pi }{6},\n$$\n\nand ${f}_{x}$ is also defined at $(1,\\sqrt{3}):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(1,\\sqrt{3})=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{{1}^{2}+(\\sqrt{3})^{2}}=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is also true. Indeed, the function $f$ itself is defined at $(0,1)$, where we have \n\n$$\nf(0,1)=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{0}{1})=0,\n$$\n\nand ${f}_{y}$ is also defined at $(0,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(0,1)=-\\frac{0}{{0}^{2}+{1}^{2}}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III\"."} :step/id #uuid "be87cbb6-8a21-5895-a193-08d9d1a311c5"}] :activity/title "Partial Differentiability of Multivariable Functions" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409538}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046659} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426724} :activity/id #uuid "5947b5e4-3c04-5937-9aec-95b10c3ffed0" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048877 :topic/id #uuid "c8e11bf4-259c-5464-94a3-b5c5d6409c00" :topic/math-academy-id 1932} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426722 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339045 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "53636330-9403-5db0-9d17-5dda7fa6783b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451561} {:answer/id #uuid "845ef4ab-3d2a-5524-947f-6cbe1fb7da82" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451562}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186451561} :answer-field/id #uuid "d3f9e12b-29cf-59f8-bf91-cc9a2f919c92" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452628} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f7b41f94-5879-57a9-a336-092f8f1aede9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451563} {:answer/id #uuid "d65c989f-2d09-5160-b064-ed15ba040057" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451564}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186451564} :answer-field/id #uuid "4fa8ff5f-ebba-5ee9-a165-1c4d49347c97" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452629} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2a5f6a07-7680-53e1-ae55-7331a6a7e9cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451565} {:answer/id #uuid "c818b116-0775-5d48-9343-c6660016175b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451566}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186451566} :answer-field/id #uuid "45930e95-82c6-577a-8b28-a081c55cca47" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452630}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "ada5bc24-8a74-58e6-b57b-4b21dfa8df4f" :question/math-academy-id "q-72022" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true about the function $f(x,y,z)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy(z-1))?$\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** The partial derivative with respect to $z$ is defined at $(3,2,1)$\n\n- **II.** ${f}_{y}(1,1,2)$ is defined\n\n- **III.** ${f}_{x}(1,2,3)$ is defined" :question/worked-solution "First, we compute the partial derivatives of $f(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{1}{xy(z-1)}\\cdot y(z-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{x} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{1}{xy(z-1)}\\cdot x(z-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{y} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂z} & =\\frac{1}{xy(z-1)}\\cdot xy \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{z-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's examine the statements one-by-one.  \n\n- Statement I is false. If we substitute $(3,2,1)$ into $f$, we get\n\n$$\nf(3,2,1)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3\\cdot 2\\cdot (1-1))=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(0),\n$$\n\nwhich is undefined.\n\n- Statement II is true. If we substitute $(1,1,2)$ into $f$, we get\n\n$$\nf(1,1,2)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1\\cdot 1\\cdot (2-1))=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1)=0,\n$$\n\nand ${f}_{y}$ is also defined at $(1,1,2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(1,1,2) & =\\frac{1}{1}=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. If we substitute $(1,2,3)$ into $f$, we get\n\n$$\nf(1,2,3)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1\\cdot 2\\cdot (3-1))=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4),\n$$\n\nand ${f}_{x}$ is also defined at $(1,2,3):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(1,2,3) & =\\frac{1}{1}=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is 'II and III  only'."} :step/id #uuid "30fe1f41-c7f8-5010-a260-ed3ef63a4abc" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426726}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426723 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268914 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "be072909-07f4-5637-903f-a70436d30a22" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449508} {:answer/id #uuid "049c935c-a4ee-5a02-b019-9fd190c4913a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449509}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186449509} :answer-field/id #uuid "548fb633-f107-5482-81d5-3893faff9da9" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450708} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6299e699-f9eb-5bb0-bad6-16b58253ff62" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449510} {:answer/id #uuid "1a54ebb4-bb0e-5d8c-89d3-811768de2241" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449511}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186449510} :answer-field/id #uuid "d8cc1d60-0069-586b-b1d2-3edcc2ab8a3a" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450709} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2c3e18ad-85a1-5207-a031-4d811fa7f0dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449512} {:answer/id #uuid "b0e9a6dd-aa8d-53df-aa34-38929292da67" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449513}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186449512} :answer-field/id #uuid "abb845e0-0f31-593e-85f2-f6c94ef89a74" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450710}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "a68d3690-fbcf-53d1-acbf-a5134efed5cb" :question/math-academy-id "q-132728" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true about the function $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-2)?$\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** Both partial derivatives exist at the point $(0,2)$\n\n- **II.** ${f}_{x}(1,0)$ is defined\n\n- **III.** ${f}_{y}(1,1)$ is defined" :question/worked-solution "First, we compute the partial derivatives of $f(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-2) \\\\  & =\\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-2} \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-2) \\\\  & =\\frac{2y}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's examine the statements one-by-one.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, the function $f$ is defined at $(0,2)$, where we have \n\n$$\nf(0,2)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡({0}^{2}+{2}^{2}-2)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡2,\n$$\n\nand both partial derivatives are also defined at $(0,2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(0,2) & =\\frac{2(0)}{{0}^{2}+{2}^{2}-2}=0 \\\\ {f}_{y}(0,2) & =\\frac{2(2)}{{0}^{2}+{2}^{2}-2}=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. If we substitute $(1,0)$ into $f$, we get\n\n$$\nf(1,0)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡({1}^{2}+{0}^{2}-2)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(-1)\n$$\n\nwhich is undefined since $\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ is defined only for $x>0.$ Since the function is not defined at the point, the derivative is also undefined.\n\n- Statement III is also false. If we substitute $(1,1)$ into $f$, we get\n\n$$\nf(1,0)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡({1}^{2}+{1}^{2}-2)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(0)\n$$\n\nwhich is undefined since $\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ is defined only for $x>0.$ Since the function is not defined at the point, the derivative is also undefined.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only\"."} :step/id #uuid "574ad7f2-698f-50d6-8eee-36f6145d16e5"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426724 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125001 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "323e1e26-806d-5103-9c5b-05be999f86f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,-1<xyz<1\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426704} {:answer/id #uuid "3f71ffa0-c375-5e54-a4b4-82c47e1862fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,0<xyz<1\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426705} {:answer/id #uuid "58a495bb-1535-5c0c-8153-9cddc1a1d72c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,xyz\\ge 0\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426707} {:answer/id #uuid "66b51739-6d9c-59ee-81d0-4f21e588a03b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,-1\\le xyz\\le 1\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426708} {:answer/id #uuid "ab77e5e6-52f4-5889-9394-0781e3a0ecd5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,xyz>0\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426711}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426704} :answer-field/id #uuid "6476017e-359c-522d-8dec-b6d7cd0832c2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122606}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "f23029a5-0003-5b31-a62a-a58e0c29de2f" :question/math-academy-id "q-36527" :question/problem "Given that $f(x,y,z)=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(xyz)$ and\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=\\frac{yz}{\\sqrt{1-(xyz)^{2}}},\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=\\frac{xz}{\\sqrt{1-(xyz)^{2}}},\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂z}=\\frac{xy}{\\sqrt{1-(xyz)^{2}}},\n$$\n\nfind the set of values $(x,y,z)$ on which all partial derivatives of $f$ exist." :question/worked-solution "First, notice that $f(x,y,z)=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(xyz)$ is defined only when $-1\\le xyz\\le 1.$ So, the domain of $f$ is \n\n$$\n{D}_{f}=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,-1\\le xyz\\le 1\\}.\n$$\n\nAs for the partial derivatives, we have the following.\n\n- The function $\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=\\frac{yz}{\\sqrt{1-(xyz)^{2}}}$ is defined only when \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1-(xyz)^{2} & >0 \\\\ (xyz)^{2} & <1 \\\\ |xyz| & <1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives $-1<xyz<1.$ So, the domain of ${f}_{x}$ is \n\n$$\n{D}_{{f}_{x}}=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,-1<xyz<1\\}.\n$$\n\n- Similarly, the domains of ${f}_{y}$ and ${f}_{z}$ are the same:\n\n$$\n{D}_{{f}_{y}}={D}_{{f}_{z}}=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,-1<xyz<1\\}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that the partial derivatives of $f$ exist on the set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{f}\\cap {D}_{{f}_{x}}\\cap {D}_{{f}_{y}}\\cap {D}_{{f}_{z}} & ={D}_{f}\\cap {D}_{{f}_{x}} \\\\  & =\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,-1\\le xyz\\le 1\\}\\cap \\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,-1<xyz<1\\} \\\\  & =\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,-1<xyz<1\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "d1a2f4bc-c2ad-5ea9-bbc9-a5f92a8d1b61" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426725}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426725 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426721 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "26545a18-833f-5099-8272-edbb2a66edaa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,y\\ne 0\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426703} {:answer/id #uuid "4d5bdc4f-f2f7-5295-b2ba-aa4588793c88" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,xy\\ge 0\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426706} {:answer/id #uuid "a9d10897-b8cf-5fad-b81e-91fbcac64c00" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "${f}_{y}$ exists everywhere on the $xy$-plane" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426710} {:answer/id #uuid "ebb0a6cd-e867-5b85-88ef-cd845e6904a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,xy<0\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426714} {:answer/id #uuid "ff439fa7-e5eb-53d6-8dc0-e5396941eb06" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,xy>0\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426717}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426717} :answer-field/id #uuid "4cca11fe-f3bb-53ee-b7b6-de2725b37ad7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426718}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "abe73768-66b7-589e-98db-9176084649e0" :question/math-academy-id "q-72006" :question/problem "Given that $f(x,y)=x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy),$ find the set of values $(x,y)$ on which $\\frac{∂f}{∂y}$ exists." :question/worked-solution "First, notice that $f(x,y)$ is defined when $xy>0.$ So, the domain of $f$ is \n\n$$\n{D}_{f}=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,xy>0\\}.\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the partial derivatives of $f(x,y)$ with respect to $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy)) \\\\  & =x\\cdot \\frac{x}{xy} \\\\  & =\\frac{x}{y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function ${f}_{y}(x,y)$ is defined only for\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y\\ne 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the domain of ${f}_{y}$ is\n\n$$\n{D}_{{f}_{y}}=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,y\\ne 0\\}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that the partial derivative of $f$ with respect to $y$ exists on the set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{f}\\cap {D}_{{f}_{y}} & =\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,xy>0\\}\\cap \\{(x,y)\\,:\\,y\\ne 0\\} \\\\  & =\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,xy>0\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "d34189a3-bcaf-5744-9e6b-ec97ad8307d1" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426722}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426726 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426720 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9a414987-4111-5550-9b84-35b48e518248" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,x\\ne 0,\\,z\\ne n\\pi \\},\\,n\\in Z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426709} {:answer/id #uuid "c4574d37-c2e2-5922-8956-3143f5bef26f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,y>0,\\,z\\ne 0\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426712} {:answer/id #uuid "c67ec9fa-9f76-59b9-b6ed-a85557705824" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,y>0,\\,z\\ne n\\pi \\},\\,n\\in Z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426713} {:answer/id #uuid "ee7e5c9b-05ac-5232-9af8-bc55f1d66335" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,y\\ne 0,\\,z\\ne 0\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426715} {:answer/id #uuid "f480f4b0-b440-57d3-be41-894b18986911" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,y\\ne 0,\\,z\\ne n\\pi \\},\\,n\\in Z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426716}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426716} :answer-field/id #uuid "4d0f4a07-8c58-5c53-b0aa-71dedf0ab601" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426719}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "13585dd1-9ab5-5887-870c-2d899c40d4a5" :question/math-academy-id "q-132652" :question/problem "Given that $f(x,y,z)=\\frac{x}{y\\operatorname{sin}⁡z}$ and\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=\\frac{1}{y\\operatorname{sin}⁡z},\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=-\\frac{x}{{y}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡z},\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂z}=-\\frac{x\\operatorname{cos}⁡z}{y{sin}^{2}⁡z}\n$$\n\nfind the set of values $(x,y,z)$ where all partial derivatives of $f$ exist." :question/worked-solution "First, notice that $f(x,y,z)=\\frac{x}{y\\operatorname{sin}⁡z}$ is defined only when $y\\ne 0$ and $z\\ne n\\pi $ where $n\\in Z.$ So, the domain of $f$ is \n\n$$\n{D}_{f}=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,y\\ne 0,z\\ne n\\pi \\},\\,n\\in Z.\n$$\n\nAs for the partial derivatives, we have the following.\n\n- The function ${f}_{x}(x,y,z)=\\frac{1}{y\\operatorname{sin}⁡z}$ is defined only when $y\\ne 0$ and $z\\ne n\\pi $ where $n\\in Z.$ So, the domain of ${f}_{x}$ is \n\n$$\n{D}_{{f}_{x}}=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,y\\ne 0,z\\ne n\\pi \\},\\,n\\in Z.\n$$\n\n- Similarly, the domains of ${f}_{y}$ and ${f}_{z}$ are the same:\n\n$$\n{D}_{{f}_{y}}={D}_{{f}_{z}}=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,y\\ne 0,z\\ne n\\pi \\},\\,n\\in Z.\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that the partials of $f$ exist on the set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{f}\\cap {D}_{{f}_{x}}\\cap {D}_{{f}_{y}}\\cap {D}_{{f}_{z}} & ={D}_{f}\\cap {D}_{{f}_{x}} \\\\  & =\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,y\\ne 0,z\\ne n\\pi \\}\\cap \\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,y\\ne 0,z\\ne n\\pi \\} \\\\  & =\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,y\\ne 0,z\\ne n\\pi \\},\\,n\\in Z.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "dd470b32-cbc1-54d2-9ca1-54631aa728a9" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426723}}] :activity/title "Partial Differentiability of Multivariable Functions" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426702}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046658} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186445259} :activity/id #uuid "2432ea49-4693-51cf-9b05-9340c356690d" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048877 :topic/id #uuid "c8e11bf4-259c-5464-94a3-b5c5d6409c00" :topic/math-academy-id 1932} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186445257 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409559 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1625c29f-0f6c-51a9-9abf-da854c690be8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,xyz>0,\\,x+y+z\\ne 0\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445247} {:answer/id #uuid "603bfd17-5e73-595d-9e4e-67eb66547b83" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,xyz>0\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445251} {:answer/id #uuid "6e5cf9f7-9ed5-55ea-b3dd-342a00251b3b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,x\\ne 0,\\,y\\ne 0,\\,z\\ne 0\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445253} {:answer/id #uuid "b33e9175-1fe3-525c-84d8-993f2fb37971" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,x+y+z>0\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445255} {:answer/id #uuid "d2f2980b-11d7-556d-9928-b285bb77ebda" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,xyz\\ge 0\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445256}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186445251} :answer-field/id #uuid "c3c16b06-37a6-5eac-b3ba-318617c8302e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409555}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "5744243e-c58b-5537-916f-bee0cb473253" :question/math-academy-id "q-132654" :question/problem "Given that $f(x,y,z)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xyz)$ and\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=\\frac{1}{x},\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=\\frac{1}{y},\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂z}=\\frac{1}{z},\n$$\n\nfind the set of values $(x,y,z)$ on which all partial derivatives of $f$ exist." :question/worked-solution "First, notice that $f(x,y,z)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xyz)$ is defined only when $xyz>0.$ So, the domain of $f$ is \n\n$$\n{D}_{f}=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,xyz>0\\}.\n$$\n\nAs for the partial derivatives, we have the following.\n\n- The function $\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=\\frac{1}{x}$ is defined only when $x\\ne 0.$ So, the domain of ${f}_{x}$ is \n\n$$\n{D}_{{f}_{x}}=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,x\\ne 0\\}.\n$$\n\n- The function $\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=\\frac{1}{y}$ is defined only when $y\\ne 0.$ So, the domain of ${f}_{y}$ is \n\n$$\n{D}_{{f}_{y}}=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,y\\ne 0\\}.\n$$\n\n- The function $\\frac{∂f}{∂z}=\\frac{1}{z}$ is defined only when $z\\ne 0.$ So, the domain of ${f}_{z}$ is \n\n$$\n{D}_{{f}_{z}}=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,z\\ne 0\\}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that the partial derivatives of $f$ exist on the set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{f}\\cap {D}_{{f}_{x}}\\cap {D}_{{f}_{y}}\\cap {D}_{{f}_{z}} & =\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,xyz>0\\}\\cap \\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,x\\ne 0\\}\\cap \\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,y\\ne 0\\}\\cap \\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,z\\ne 0\\} \\\\  & =\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,xyz>0\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "1b612f2a-e93c-5ad0-972f-c5368a3312ef" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186445260}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186445258 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268914 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "be072909-07f4-5637-903f-a70436d30a22" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449508} {:answer/id #uuid "049c935c-a4ee-5a02-b019-9fd190c4913a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449509}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186449509} :answer-field/id #uuid "548fb633-f107-5482-81d5-3893faff9da9" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450708} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6299e699-f9eb-5bb0-bad6-16b58253ff62" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449510} {:answer/id #uuid "1a54ebb4-bb0e-5d8c-89d3-811768de2241" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449511}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186449510} :answer-field/id #uuid "d8cc1d60-0069-586b-b1d2-3edcc2ab8a3a" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450709} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2c3e18ad-85a1-5207-a031-4d811fa7f0dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449512} {:answer/id #uuid "b0e9a6dd-aa8d-53df-aa34-38929292da67" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449513}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186449512} :answer-field/id #uuid "abb845e0-0f31-593e-85f2-f6c94ef89a74" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450710}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "a68d3690-fbcf-53d1-acbf-a5134efed5cb" :question/math-academy-id "q-132728" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true about the function $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-2)?$\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** Both partial derivatives exist at the point $(0,2)$\n\n- **II.** ${f}_{x}(1,0)$ is defined\n\n- **III.** ${f}_{y}(1,1)$ is defined" :question/worked-solution "First, we compute the partial derivatives of $f(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-2) \\\\  & =\\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-2} \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-2) \\\\  & =\\frac{2y}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's examine the statements one-by-one.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, the function $f$ is defined at $(0,2)$, where we have \n\n$$\nf(0,2)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡({0}^{2}+{2}^{2}-2)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡2,\n$$\n\nand both partial derivatives are also defined at $(0,2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(0,2) & =\\frac{2(0)}{{0}^{2}+{2}^{2}-2}=0 \\\\ {f}_{y}(0,2) & =\\frac{2(2)}{{0}^{2}+{2}^{2}-2}=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. If we substitute $(1,0)$ into $f$, we get\n\n$$\nf(1,0)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡({1}^{2}+{0}^{2}-2)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(-1)\n$$\n\nwhich is undefined since $\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ is defined only for $x>0.$ Since the function is not defined at the point, the derivative is also undefined.\n\n- Statement III is also false. If we substitute $(1,1)$ into $f$, we get\n\n$$\nf(1,0)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡({1}^{2}+{1}^{2}-2)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(0)\n$$\n\nwhich is undefined since $\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ is defined only for $x>0.$ Since the function is not defined at the point, the derivative is also undefined.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only\"."} :step/id #uuid "5a19d090-fc30-5d59-972c-c7b1fd1ca305"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186445259 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382207 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "32e88f5b-98b5-56cf-80ff-82851c3303b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,xy\\ge 0,z\\ne 0\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445248} {:answer/id #uuid "395e6ad4-f871-5195-aed0-c82a5612a79c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,xy>0,z>0\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445249} {:answer/id #uuid "410d16c0-a17f-5bb6-8557-36dd6a7c08d7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,xy\\ne 0,z<0\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445250} {:answer/id #uuid "629c6583-5abe-5cd2-8098-b6cded7ee4e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,xy>0,z\\ne 0\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445252} {:answer/id #uuid "a318ea21-95fd-5a51-9e1e-c2501bcf0d2e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,xy=0,z\\ne 0\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445254}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186445252} :answer-field/id #uuid "bb805034-5a71-5881-8d6e-dc2faac4f6f6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382208}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "07b7a4d5-0d88-5759-b677-08d7e8ac3fca" :question/math-academy-id "q-72007" :question/problem "Given that $f(x,y,z)=\\frac{{e}^{\\sqrt{xy}}}{z}$ and\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=\\frac{y{e}^{\\sqrt{xy}}}{2z\\sqrt{xy}},\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=\\frac{x{e}^{\\sqrt{xy}}}{2z\\sqrt{xy}},\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂z}=-\\frac{{e}^{\\sqrt{xy}}}{{z}^{2}},\n$$\n\nfind the set of values $(x,y,z)$ on which all partial derivatives of $f$ exist." :question/worked-solution "First, notice that $f(x,y,z)=\\frac{{e}^{\\sqrt{xy}}}{z}$ is defined only when $xy\\ge 0$ and $z\\ne 0.$ So, the domain of $f$ is \n\n$$\n{D}_{f}=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,xy\\ge 0,z\\ne 0\\}.\n$$\n\nAs for the partial derivatives, we have the following.\n\n- The function ${f}_{x}=\\frac{y{e}^{\\sqrt{xy}}}{2z\\sqrt{xy}}$ is defined only when $xy>0$ and $z\\ne 0.$ So, the domain of ${f}_{x}$ is \n\n$$\n{D}_{{f}_{x}}=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,xy>0,z\\ne 0\\}.\n$$\n\n- Similarly, the domain of ${f}_{y}=\\frac{x{e}^{\\sqrt{xy}}}{2z\\sqrt{xy}}$ is the same\n\n$$\n{D}_{{f}_{y}}=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,xy>0,z\\ne 0\\}.\n$$\n\n- The function ${f}_{z}=-\\frac{{e}^{\\sqrt{xy}}}{{z}^{2}}$ is defined only when $xy\\ge 0$ and $z\\ne 0.$ So, the domain of ${f}_{z}$ is\n\n$$\n{D}_{{f}_{z}}=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,xy\\ge 0,z\\ne 0\\}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that the partials of $f$ exist on the set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{f}\\cap {D}_{{f}_{x}}\\cap {D}_{{f}_{y}}\\cap {D}_{{f}_{z}} & ={D}_{f}\\cap {D}_{{f}_{x}} \\\\  & =\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,xy\\ge 0,z\\ne 0\\}\\cap \\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,xy>0,z\\ne 0\\} \\\\  & =\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,xy>0,z\\ne 0\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "5c9fcb15-a332-5a35-8e19-9f260bf4cc92" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186445261}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186445260 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268907 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9fc5f850-64cd-5893-8891-369f969391e9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449502} {:answer/id #uuid "9c7c52c9-c826-5b11-9b7a-993392ba4ad3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449503}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186449503} :answer-field/id #uuid "e0737846-2cd6-5da0-9650-75bc9876d5a8" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450705} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8b64dfe9-6cec-50a9-80d9-4a4526e80263" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449504} {:answer/id #uuid "6f04e2aa-8d8b-5312-ad91-326d2690aab0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449505}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186449504} :answer-field/id #uuid "2de809ae-338b-5353-b9c7-b997f94a0161" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450706} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "edd0b63c-f4f5-5c2b-bd85-fa00f3b0bca4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449506} {:answer/id #uuid "6a6ab58d-d0b1-5f84-9b1f-39c31befd21b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449507}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186449507} :answer-field/id #uuid "6f8beae8-a1f2-5630-bd94-336847d03d63" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450707}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "9dc50a8a-45d6-510c-8209-df3404b44621" :question/math-academy-id "q-132680" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true about the function $f(x,y)=\\frac{\\sqrt{x}}{{y}^{2}}?$\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** Both partial derivatives exist at the point $(4,2)$\n\n- **II.** ${f}_{x}(0,2)$ is defined\n\n- **III.** ${f}_{y}(0,2)$ is defined" :question/worked-solution "First, we compute the partial derivatives of $f(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{\\sqrt{x}}{{y}^{2}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\sqrt{x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2{y}^{2}\\sqrt{x}} \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{\\sqrt{x}}{{y}^{2}}) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{x}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{x}\\cdot (-\\frac{2}{{y}^{3}}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2\\sqrt{x}}{{y}^{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's examine the statements one-by-one.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, the function $f$ is defined at $(4,2)$, where we have \n\n$$\nf(4,2)=\\frac{\\sqrt{4}}{{2}^{2}}=\\frac{1}{2},\n$$\n\nand both partial derivatives are also defined at $(4,2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(4,2) & =\\frac{1}{2({2}^{2})\\sqrt{4}}=\\frac{1}{16} \\\\ {f}_{y}(4,2) & =-\\frac{2\\sqrt{4}}{{2}^{3}}=-\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. If we substitute $(0,2)$ into ${f}_{x}$, we get\n\n$$\n{f}_{x}(0,2)=\\frac{1}{2({2}^{2})\\sqrt{0}}=\\frac{1}{0},\n$$\n\nwhich is undefined since we have division by zero.\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, the function $f$ itself is defined at $(0,2)$, where we have \n\n$$\nf(0,2)=\\frac{\\sqrt{0}}{{2}^{2}}=0,\n$$\n\nand ${f}_{y}$ is also defined at $(0,2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(0,2)=-\\frac{2\\sqrt{0}}{{2}^{3}}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only\"."} :step/id #uuid "7b68aec1-f3e3-5aa1-bec7-f5fcbb362752" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186445258}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186445261 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382221 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "45d1ed56-bfc6-5bda-a5c6-d5385ab5bd77" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453940} {:answer/id #uuid "bf41f840-b49c-5384-a274-dfe536fcb33d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453941}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186453941} :answer-field/id #uuid "edafd0ac-42e0-5d4a-be7b-7aa902ffa1b6" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454720} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "060c118c-98d6-5841-94fb-d05d803c937b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453942} {:answer/id #uuid "3ca503bc-9f0b-57b9-9107-116d82172e36" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453943}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186453942} :answer-field/id #uuid "97a453da-0bfb-5722-95ac-96c9ea20e34e" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454721} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4b8b8bc4-d09b-5051-b032-eb64e490a6af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453944} {:answer/id #uuid "5922a222-d689-5f69-94af-6d12b904bec7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453945}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186453944} :answer-field/id #uuid "5e81b99c-60a0-5dd3-8161-a53cd384fae9" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454722}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "ff98942c-b72f-558c-86fd-786f6575a16d" :question/math-academy-id "q-72019" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true about the function $f(x,y,z)=\\sqrt{2x+y-z}?$\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** ${f}_{y}(1,1,1)$ is defined\n\n- **II.** ${f}_{z}(0,1,2)$ is defined\n\n- **III.** The partial derivative with respect to $x$ is defined at $(0,1,1)$" :question/worked-solution "First, we compute the partial derivatives of $f(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{2x+y-z}}\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2x+y-z}} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{2x+y-z}}\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{2x+y-z}} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂z} & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{2x+y-z}}\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{2x+y-z}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's examine the statements one-by-one.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, the function $f$ is defined at $(1,1,1)$, where we have \n\n$$\nf(1,1,1)=\\sqrt{2\\cdot 1+1-1}=\\sqrt{2},\n$$\n\nand ${f}_{y}$ is also defined at $(1,1,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(1,1,1) & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{2\\cdot 1+1-1}}=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. If we substitute $(0,1,2)$ into $f$, we get\n\n$$\nf(0,1,2)=\\sqrt{2\\cdot 0+1-2}=\\sqrt{-1},\n$$\n\nwhich is undefined since the number under the radical is negative.\n\n- Statement III is false. If we substitute $(0,1,1)$ into ${f}_{x}$, we get\n\n$$\n{f}_{x}(0,1,1)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2\\cdot 0+1-1}}=\\frac{1}{0},\n$$\n\nwhich is undefined since we have division by zero.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is 'I only'."} :step/id #uuid "c05be20d-2e9f-5518-9fbb-273892668ed2" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186445257}}] :activity/title "Partial Differentiability of Multivariable Functions" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445246}]}
